// markdownlint-cli2 — structural linting for the instruction docs that are canon here.
//
// **Why this is here.** `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and
// `.github/copilot-instructions.md` are a hand-maintained three-way mirror that
// three different tools read, and nothing checked their structure. The first
// scoped run found a dead cross-reference in `CLAUDE.md` pointing at
// `#local-persistence` — an anchor that resolves in `AGENTS.md`, whose heading is
// a bare `### Local persistence`, and did not in `CLAUDE.md`, whose heading had
// grown a parenthetical. The mirror had drifted at a heading and broken a link in
// the file loaded into every session. It also found `"New: <Type>"` in
// `docs/wagtail-content-mapping.md`, where the placeholder is parsed as an HTML
// tag and vanishes from the rendered page.
//
// **This file carries rules only — never the file list.** `bun run lint:docs`
// derives that from `git ls-files`, for reasons `build_scripts/lint-docs.js`
// states at length. A `globs` key here would be a second, competing answer to
// the question that script exists to answer.
{
  "config": {
    // Off, and NOT because Prettier owns it. `.prettierrc.json` sets no
    // `proseWrap`, so Prettier's default `"preserve"` applies and it never
    // reflows markdown prose — only fenced code inside it. `printWidth: 100`
    // therefore says nothing about the lines this rule measures, and a comment
    // claiming otherwise would be a wrong reason sitting where a checked one
    // belongs.
    //
    // The honest reason is the measurement: at MD013's default 80 this reported
    // 1,066 findings, and at `line_length: 100` with tables and code blocks
    // exempt it still reported 777. Reflowing several thousand lines of canon
    // prose to satisfy a width nothing else enforces is churn, and churn in
    // these three files is what the mirror can least afford.
    "MD013": false,

    // `<br>` is how a markdown table holds more than one line in a cell, and
    // `docs/sfgov-live-design-inspiration.md` uses it throughout. Every OTHER
    // inline element stays reportable, which is the half that earns its keep:
    // an angle-bracket placeholder written outside backticks — `<Type>`,
    // `<specific failure>` — is parsed as an HTML tag and silently dropped from
    // the rendered page. Turning this rule off wholesale to accommodate the
    // tables would have taken that catch with it.
    "MD033": { "allowed_elements": ["br"] },

    // `docs/karl-mockup-cookbook.md` is one recipe per Karl content type, and
    // each recipe legitimately carries its own "The recipe", "Traps" and "The
    // Karl form, top to bottom". `siblings_only` still catches a true duplicate
    // under one parent while allowing the repeated shape across sections.
    "MD024": { "siblings_only": true },

    // The dated research notes under `docs/` number their findings as bold
    // paragraph leads — `**5. Raw field names — …**` — eight in a row in
    // `karl-live-admin-verification-2026-07-06.md` alone. This rule fired on two
    // of those eight, so satisfying it would mean converting two items of a
    // uniform list into headings and leaving six behind. Those notes are records
    // rather than documentation (see AGENTS.md), and a rule that can only be
    // satisfied by making a record internally inconsistent is the rule that is
    // wrong here.
    "MD036": false,

    // A skill file opens with YAML frontmatter and then a `###` heading, which
    // is the format the skill loader expects rather than a mistake.
    "MD041": false,
  },
}
